---
description: 
globs: *.rs
alwaysApply: false
---
Every Rust file, but especially library files, should contain a `mod test {}` that primarily runs unit tests on the methods of the file. DO NOT TEST TRIVIAL FUNCTIONALITY.
After every change, run the tests and make sure the tests pass. If a test fails, check whether the test is wrong or the code was wrongfully changed. Prefer to not change tests unless the functionality of the method was fundamentally changed.
Also, there musn't be any test-specific code mixed in production code. This must be avoided at all costs and is also valid for `cfg(test)` and more!

If applicable, integration tests should not be part of the library file but of the file where the parts of the library are used.
Ask how to proceed with tests if a third-party tool (such as an external library wrapping a command or HTTP requests) or external data would be used by the test.

